1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,439
Your CTO asks: "A developer wanted
to keep one file out of a commit.

2
00:00:08,479 --> 00:00:11,892
The commit deleted that file
from the project for everyone.

3
00:00:11,932 --> 00:00:15,111
What did they type, and why
did Git itself suggest it?"

4
00:00:15,151 --> 00:00:16,984
Make your guess before I answer.

5
00:00:21,824 --> 00:00:27,197
They typed git rm --cached. And Git did
suggest it: in a repository without commits,

6
00:00:27,237 --> 00:00:30,999
git status prints that exact
command as the way to unstage.

7
00:00:31,039 --> 00:00:34,866
There the advice is right. It's
also where the habit starts.

8
00:00:35,206 --> 00:00:40,317
git rm --cached deletes an index entry.
The index is Git's list of what goes into

9
00:00:40,357 --> 00:00:45,215
the next commit, one entry for each file.
Unstaging means restoring an entry.

10
00:00:45,255 --> 00:00:50,303
Those are the same thing in exactly one
situation, and different in every other.

11
00:00:50,343 --> 00:00:55,518
Today we sort out which command does what
to an index entry, so that you choose by

12
00:00:55,558 --> 00:00:58,953
effect and not by habit.
Watch for that one situation.

13
00:00:58,993 --> 00:01:00,797
It's a single row of a table.

14
00:01:02,637 --> 00:01:06,093
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

15
00:01:06,133 --> 00:01:10,908
You know that the index is a full snapshot,
and you know how to stage content, whole

16
00:01:10,948 --> 00:01:15,607
or in part. Here's the five-second reminder:
you edit in the working tree, git add

17
00:01:15,647 --> 00:01:19,103
copies a version into the index,
and HEAD is the last commit.

18
00:01:19,143 --> 00:01:22,313
This video completes the set
of operations on the index.

19
00:01:22,653 --> 00:01:27,178
Four parts. How deletions, renames
and binary files look in the index.

20
00:01:27,218 --> 00:01:30,635
What the bulk forms of git
add take in: ., -u and -A.

21
00:01:30,675 --> 00:01:35,266
Three commands that people use to
unstage, with two different meanings.

22
00:01:35,306 --> 00:01:40,679
And two forms of git commit that take content
from the working tree and bypass what

23
00:01:40,719 --> 00:01:41,397
you staged.

24
00:01:43,237 --> 00:01:44,815
Here's what you'll walk away with.

25
00:01:45,155 --> 00:01:46,567
After this video you can:

26
00:01:46,907 --> 00:01:51,798
Stage a deletion, a rename and a binary
change, and read each in git status.

27
00:01:52,138 --> 00:01:57,713
Predict what git add ., git add -u and
git add -A stage from a given directory.

28
00:01:58,053 --> 00:02:03,963
Choose between git restore --staged, git
reset <path> and git rm --cached for a

29
00:02:04,003 --> 00:02:04,791
given path.

30
00:02:05,131 --> 00:02:09,314
Explain how git commit -a and git
commit <path> bypass what was staged.

31
00:02:11,154 --> 00:02:16,521
Deletions, renames, binary files. In one
sentence: in index terms a deletion is a

32
00:02:16,561 --> 00:02:21,995
removed entry, a rename is a removed entry
plus an added one, and a binary file is

33
00:02:22,035 --> 00:02:26,267
an ordinary entry whose content
Git will neither show nor split.

34
00:02:26,607 --> 00:02:30,745
After rm, the entry exists and
the file doesn't: a space and D.

35
00:02:30,785 --> 00:02:34,791
git add on the missing path
removes the entry: D and a space.

36
00:02:34,831 --> 00:02:40,162
git rm does both steps at once. For a
rename, status prints R, but the index only

37
00:02:40,202 --> 00:02:45,601
lost one entry and gained another with the
same blob ID, the ID of the stored file

38
00:02:45,641 --> 00:02:51,171
content. Nothing recorded a rename: status
inferred it from the pair, as you learned

39
00:02:51,211 --> 00:02:51,967
in video 13.

40
00:02:52,307 --> 00:02:57,656
For binary content, such as an image: unless
a diff attribute decides, Git inspects

41
00:02:57,696 --> 00:03:02,265
the content. The manual says "if it
looks like text and is smaller than

42
00:03:02,305 --> 00:03:07,718
core.bigFileThreshold, it is treated as
text". For a binary file, the diff says that

43
00:03:07,758 --> 00:03:11,224
the files differ, and git
add -p has nothing to offer.

44
00:03:11,264 --> 00:03:15,314
When one byte changes, the next
add writes a complete new blob.

45
00:03:15,654 --> 00:03:20,326
The scope of git add. In one sentence: the
bulk forms differ in two things: whether

46
00:03:20,366 --> 00:03:24,811
untracked files are included, and whether
the command is limited to the current

47
00:03:24,851 --> 00:03:28,387
directory. An untracked file is
one with no entry in the index.

48
00:03:28,727 --> 00:03:34,403
git add -u: modified tracked files staged,
deleted tracked files staged, untracked

49
00:03:34,443 --> 00:03:37,818
files not touched. Scope,
the whole working tree.

50
00:03:38,158 --> 00:03:42,477
git add -A: modified, deleted
and untracked, all staged.

51
00:03:42,517 --> 00:03:44,812
Scope, the whole working tree.

52
00:03:45,152 --> 00:03:48,556
git add .: modified, deleted
and untracked, all staged.

53
00:03:48,596 --> 00:03:50,998
Scope, the current directory and below.

54
00:03:51,338 --> 00:03:55,826
git add -u .: like -u, limited to
the current directory and below.

55
00:03:56,166 --> 00:04:01,527
git add --no-all <path>: modified staged,
deletions not touched, untracked staged.

56
00:04:01,567 --> 00:04:05,348
Scope, the pathspec, meaning
the path or pattern you name.

57
00:04:05,688 --> 00:04:12,390
No form adds ignored files. All forms are
🟢 SAFE, and all have a preview: --dry-run,

58
00:04:12,430 --> 00:04:12,872
or -n.

59
00:04:13,212 --> 00:04:16,849
Quick quiz, three options.
You're in a subdirectory.

60
00:04:16,889 --> 00:04:22,647
You want to stage your edits to tracked
files across the whole project, and no new

61
00:04:22,687 --> 00:04:26,961
files. git add ., git add -u,
or git add -A? Say your answer.

62
00:04:31,801 --> 00:04:37,198
git add -u. The dot stays in the current
directory, and capital A takes new files

63
00:04:37,238 --> 00:04:37,467
too.

64
00:04:37,807 --> 00:04:43,342
An outdated-advice note from the textbook:
tutorials written before Git 2.0 say that

65
00:04:43,382 --> 00:04:48,850
git add -u and git add -A cover only the
current directory, and that git add <path>

66
00:04:48,890 --> 00:04:51,704
ignores removed files.
Both changed in 2.0.

67
00:04:52,044 --> 00:04:57,536
Unstaging: three commands, two meanings.
In one sentence: to unstage a path is to

68
00:04:57,576 --> 00:05:02,931
make its index entry equal to HEAD's
again. git restore --staged <path> and git

69
00:05:02,971 --> 00:05:08,464
reset <path> do that. git rm --cached
<path> deletes the entry, which is the same

70
00:05:08,504 --> 00:05:13,996
thing only when HEAD has no such path. All
three are 🟡 CAUTION: they change index

71
00:05:14,036 --> 00:05:16,182
entries, and files on disk stay.

72
00:05:16,522 --> 00:05:21,210
A path that HEAD has: git restore
--staged undoes the staged change.

73
00:05:21,250 --> 00:05:26,563
git reset <path> undoes the staged change.
git rm --cached stages a deletion.

74
00:05:26,903 --> 00:05:30,192
A path that HEAD lacks: all
three remove the entry.

75
00:05:30,532 --> 00:05:36,388
A repository without commits: git restore
--staged fails, because HEAD can't be

76
00:05:36,428 --> 00:05:40,046
resolved. git reset works.
git rm --cached works.

77
00:05:40,386 --> 00:05:45,380
Safety check: the first two have none.
git rm --cached refuses when the staged

78
00:05:45,420 --> 00:05:50,801
content matches neither HEAD nor the file.
That guard protects a staged version that

79
00:05:50,841 --> 00:05:56,093
would otherwise survive only as an unreachable
object, one that nothing points to.

80
00:05:56,433 --> 00:06:01,474
git reset without a path unstages everything.
And when the working tree has moved on

81
00:06:01,514 --> 00:06:06,011
since the add, the unreachable blob is
the only copy of the staged version.

82
00:06:06,351 --> 00:06:11,031
Two commit forms that bypass what you staged.
In one sentence: git commit -a stages

83
00:06:11,071 --> 00:06:15,523
every modified and deleted tracked file
and then commits, and git commit <path>

84
00:06:15,563 --> 00:06:19,447
commits the working-tree content of
the named paths and nothing else.

85
00:06:19,487 --> 00:06:23,428
Both read the working tree at commit
time, whatever you staged before.

86
00:06:23,468 --> 00:06:27,749
Both are 🟢 SAFE in the textbook's table,
and both have a preview: --dry-run.

87
00:06:28,089 --> 00:06:33,046
Precisely, from the manual: with paths,
the commit takes "the updated working tree

88
00:06:33,086 --> 00:06:38,103
contents of the paths specified on the
command line, disregarding any contents that

89
00:06:38,143 --> 00:06:43,161
have been staged for other paths". -i, or
--include, instead stages the named paths

90
00:06:43,201 --> 00:06:47,731
on top of what is already staged,
"usually not what you want unless you are

91
00:06:47,771 --> 00:06:49,620
concluding a conflicted merge".

92
00:06:49,960 --> 00:06:54,861
So, three cases. With git commit -a, the
tree of the new commit is the index after

93
00:06:54,901 --> 00:06:59,801
staging every tracked modification and
deletion, and staged changes to other paths

94
00:06:59,841 --> 00:07:04,742
are committed. With git commit <path>, it's
HEAD's tree with the named paths taken

95
00:07:04,782 --> 00:07:09,562
from the working tree, and staged changes
to other paths stay staged and are not

96
00:07:09,602 --> 00:07:14,563
committed. With git commit -i <path>, it's
the index after staging the named paths.

97
00:07:14,903 --> 00:07:20,130
When staging adds nothing. The textbook
is fair to -a: one finished change in an

98
00:07:20,170 --> 00:07:25,462
otherwise clean working tree, and git
commit -a after reading git status is fine.

99
00:07:25,502 --> 00:07:30,333
The index earns its keep when the
working tree holds more than one change.

100
00:07:30,373 --> 00:07:35,731
After git add -p, -a is the wrong reflex:
it stages again every hunk you left out.

101
00:07:37,571 --> 00:07:42,246
Go back to the packing list from video
15, the index as a shipping list.

102
00:07:42,286 --> 00:07:46,502
Every operation in this video is
an edit to one line of the list.

103
00:07:46,842 --> 00:07:51,997
Staging a deletion: strike the line. Staging
a rename: strike one line, and write

104
00:07:52,037 --> 00:07:57,000
another with the same item number.
Unstaging: copy the line back from the last

105
00:07:57,040 --> 00:08:02,132
shipment's list. git rm --cached: strike
the line. If the last shipment had that

106
00:08:02,172 --> 00:08:07,135
item, a struck line means "do not ship
it any more", and that's a deletion for

107
00:08:07,175 --> 00:08:09,380
everyone who receives the next box.

108
00:08:09,720 --> 00:08:13,671
Where it breaks: git commit -a and git
commit <path> don't read your list as you

109
00:08:13,711 --> 00:08:17,562
left it. They rewrite lines from the
shelf, at the last moment, and then ship.

110
00:08:19,402 --> 00:08:22,866
Read the middle row first: for
a new path, all three agree.

111
00:08:22,906 --> 00:08:27,439
That's the one situation I asked you to
watch for, and that row is why people

112
00:08:27,479 --> 00:08:32,012
believe the commands are interchangeable.
The top right cell is the incident.

113
00:08:32,352 --> 00:08:37,296
That's the incident from the hook, line
by line. Read the line marked mechanism.

114
00:08:41,636 --> 00:08:45,144
After rm, a space and D: the
entry exists, the file doesn't.

115
00:08:45,184 --> 00:08:48,752
git add on the missing path
removes the entry: D and a space.

116
00:08:49,092 --> 00:08:53,136
A manual mv, then both paths
are added. Status prints R.

117
00:08:53,176 --> 00:08:58,607
The index lost one entry and gained
another with the same blob ID, 67b3f03.

118
00:08:58,947 --> 00:09:04,178
A small image is staged like any other
file. The entry looks like any other.

119
00:09:04,218 --> 00:09:05,843
--stat gives byte sizes.

120
00:09:06,183 --> 00:09:09,168
One byte changes, and there
are no hunks to choose from.

121
00:09:09,508 --> 00:09:14,448
We're inside src/, with one modification,
one deletion and one new file both inside

122
00:09:14,488 --> 00:09:19,369
and outside that directory. --dry-run prints
what would happen and stages nothing.

123
00:09:19,709 --> 00:09:24,748
Try it now, on paper, thirty seconds.
Six changed paths are on screen.

124
00:09:24,788 --> 00:09:28,957
Write down the ones git add .
would stage from inside src.

125
00:09:28,997 --> 00:09:33,892
Then do the same for -u and for -A.
Pause me, and write your answer.

126
00:09:48,432 --> 00:09:54,329
Check your lists. . stayed inside src/,
and included the new file and the removal.

127
00:09:54,369 --> 00:10:00,267
-u reached README.md and docs/guide.md
outside the directory, and skipped both new

128
00:10:00,307 --> 00:10:05,408
files. -A took everything. If the
removal surprised you, that's normal.

129
00:10:05,748 --> 00:10:11,072
And --no-all with a pathspec: new and
modified files, but removals are left

130
00:10:11,112 --> 00:10:11,715
unstaged.

131
00:10:14,555 --> 00:10:19,293
A tracked file with a staged modification,
and a new file that has only been added.

132
00:10:19,333 --> 00:10:23,840
Predict what each of the three commands
leaves in the index for each of the two

133
00:10:23,880 --> 00:10:24,185
paths.

134
00:10:24,525 --> 00:10:26,822
git restore --staged 🟡 CAUTION:

135
00:10:27,162 --> 00:10:33,063
The tracked file's entry holds HEAD's blob
1e0b1ad again, and the path shows a space

136
00:10:33,103 --> 00:10:38,084
and M. The new file, which HEAD lacks,
lost its entry and is untracked.

137
00:10:38,424 --> 00:10:39,870
git reset <path> 🟡 CAUTION:

138
00:10:40,210 --> 00:10:43,628
The identical index, and a
report of what remains unstaged.

139
00:10:43,968 --> 00:10:49,153
git rm --cached 🟡 CAUTION. Predict
the status code for the tracked file.

140
00:10:49,193 --> 00:10:51,113
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

141
00:10:55,953 --> 00:11:01,258
git rm --cached removed both entries. For
the new file the outcome is the same.

142
00:11:01,298 --> 00:11:06,738
For the tracked file it's a staged deletion,
D and a space: the next commit would

143
00:11:06,778 --> 00:11:12,084
remove src/retriever.py from the project,
although the file stays on your disk.

144
00:11:12,424 --> 00:11:17,623
The guard: when index, HEAD and working
tree hold three different versions of one

145
00:11:17,663 --> 00:11:19,564
file, git rm --cached refuses.

146
00:11:19,904 --> 00:11:25,614
And the repository with no commit yet. git
status itself recommends git rm --cached

147
00:11:25,654 --> 00:11:30,810
here, and git restore --staged fails,
because there's no HEAD to copy from.

148
00:11:30,850 --> 00:11:36,422
Every staged path is new, so the advice
is right. This is where the habit starts.

149
00:11:36,762 --> 00:11:42,805
One modified file, one deleted
file, one new file. Nothing staged.

150
00:11:42,845 --> 00:11:48,519
Predict what git commit -a records.
Two of them, or all three?

151
00:11:48,859 --> 00:11:52,581
Two. The modification and the
deletion. Not the new file.

152
00:11:52,921 --> 00:11:57,710
settings.yaml is staged, and
retriever.py is modified and not staged.

153
00:11:57,750 --> 00:12:03,379
The path form committed retriever.py alone,
and left the staged settings.yaml for

154
00:12:03,419 --> 00:12:03,799
later.

155
00:12:04,139 --> 00:12:10,044
Now the surprise. A clean version of
retriever.py is staged, and the working tree

156
00:12:10,084 --> 00:12:16,208
holds one more line, a debug line. Predict
what git commit src/retriever.py records.

157
00:12:16,248 --> 00:12:18,189
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

158
00:12:26,029 --> 00:12:30,633
The commit contains the debug line. What
you staged for that path was replaced by

159
00:12:30,673 --> 00:12:31,607
the file on disk.

160
00:12:31,947 --> 00:12:36,288
-i adds the named path on top of what
is staged: two files in the commit.

161
00:12:36,628 --> 00:12:39,528
A path that Git doesn't know
is an error, not an add.

162
00:12:39,868 --> 00:12:44,379
And --dry-run previews any commit
command without creating the commit.

163
00:12:46,219 --> 00:12:47,867
Five mistakes to watch for.

164
00:12:48,207 --> 00:12:53,189
A commit deleted a file you meant to unstage.
Root cause: git rm --cached was used

165
00:12:53,229 --> 00:12:58,026
on a path that HEAD has; it removes the
entry instead of restoring the one from

166
00:12:58,066 --> 00:12:58,332
HEAD.

167
00:12:58,672 --> 00:13:03,804
The commit contains a line you left out
with -p. Root cause: it was made with -a or

168
00:13:03,844 --> 00:13:07,480
with a path, and both read the
working tree at commit time.

169
00:13:07,820 --> 00:13:11,334
git add . in a subdirectory
missed changes elsewhere.

170
00:13:11,374 --> 00:13:16,968
Root cause: . is a pathspec for the current
directory and below; -u and -A cover the

171
00:13:17,008 --> 00:13:18,243
whole working tree.

172
00:13:18,583 --> 00:13:24,735
An .env or a dataset became tracked by
accident. Root cause: git add -A and git add

173
00:13:24,775 --> 00:13:30,629
. stage untracked files; use -u for files
Git already knows, and name new files

174
00:13:30,669 --> 00:13:31,449
explicitly.

175
00:13:31,789 --> 00:13:36,182
git commit <path> left other staged
work behind. Root cause: the path form

176
00:13:36,222 --> 00:13:40,973
disregards contents staged for other paths;
they stay staged for a later commit.

177
00:13:42,813 --> 00:13:48,063
Now, out of the lab. A developer on a
backend team stages a configuration file by

178
00:13:48,103 --> 00:13:53,353
accident, notices it in git status, and
runs the command they remember from their

179
00:13:53,393 --> 00:13:57,076
first day with Git: git rm
--cached config/settings.yaml.

180
00:13:57,116 --> 00:14:02,366
Status now shows the path twice, as D in
the first column and as untracked, which

181
00:14:02,406 --> 00:14:07,655
they read as "unstaged". They commit and
push. On the next pull, every teammate's

182
00:14:07,695 --> 00:14:12,945
settings.yaml is deleted from the working
tree, and the service fails to start on

183
00:14:12,985 --> 00:14:13,860
three laptops.

184
00:14:14,200 --> 00:14:20,160
The fix after the commit, from the textbook:
git restore --source=HEAD~1 --staged

185
00:14:20,200 --> 00:14:26,161
<path>, then a new commit. The prevention:
unstage with git restore --staged, and

186
00:14:26,201 --> 00:14:30,680
read D in the first column as
"the next commit deletes this".

187
00:14:32,520 --> 00:14:38,178
Your turn. Do Lab 2.4, "Unstage three
ways and compare the results", in

188
00:14:38,218 --> 00:14:41,549
lab-manual/m02-working-tree-index-head.md.

189
00:14:41,889 --> 00:14:46,895
Before each of the three commands, predict
the output of git status --short and of

190
00:14:46,935 --> 00:14:51,572
git ls-files --stage for both paths.
Before the step in a repository without

191
00:14:51,612 --> 00:14:54,957
commits, predict which of the
three commands will fail.

192
00:14:56,797 --> 00:15:02,405
Q28: "Compare git restore --staged, git
reset <path> and git rm --cached for a path

193
00:15:02,445 --> 00:15:07,850
that HEAD has, for a path that HEAD lacks,
and in a repository without commits."

194
00:15:10,190 --> 00:15:14,875
Answer out loud, as a three-by-three table.
A strong answer defines "unstage" in

195
00:15:14,915 --> 00:15:19,836
terms of the index entry and HEAD first,
and derives each cell from that definition.

196
00:15:19,876 --> 00:15:24,620
It names the one cell that causes incidents,
and the safety check that one of the

197
00:15:24,660 --> 00:15:26,923
three commands has and the others lack.

198
00:15:28,763 --> 00:15:34,248
Let's land this. You should now be able to
say, in your own words: a staged deletion

199
00:15:34,288 --> 00:15:39,641
is a removed index entry, and a staged
rename is a removed entry plus an added one

200
00:15:39,681 --> 00:15:44,903
with the same blob. git add . is limited
to the current directory, -u covers all

201
00:15:44,943 --> 00:15:48,586
tracked files and no new ones,
and -A covers everything.

202
00:15:48,926 --> 00:15:54,469
Unstaging means making the entry equal to
HEAD's: git restore --staged and git reset

203
00:15:54,509 --> 00:15:59,985
<path> do that, and git rm --cached removes
the entry, which stages a deletion when

204
00:16:00,025 --> 00:16:01,181
HEAD has the path.

205
00:16:01,521 --> 00:16:06,276
git commit -a and git commit <path> take
